    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.3389/feart.2026.1761959",
    "abstract": "Antarctic land ice stores the majority of Earth\u2019s freshwater and carries substantial uncertainties regarding its future contribution to global sea level rise. While ocean processes associated with basal melting currently dominate ice loss, atmospheric forcing could have an increasing future impact, especially with intensified extreme weather events. For instance, atmospheric rivers, which are key drivers of long-distance moisture transport, introduce significant uncertainties to Antarctica\u2019s ice mass balance, as they are capable of causing both intense snowfall and surface melting. They also impact ocean stratification and mixed-layer depth through freshwater input, ultimately affecting air-sea exchange. Associated interactions among components of the Earth system\u2014atmosphere, ocean, and glacier\u2014are not fully captured by global climate models and observations. This paper assesses Antarctica\u2019s future, highlighting uncertainties stemming from limited understanding of atmospheric and oceanic forcings such as atmospheric rivers, and their consequences for projecting sea-level rise-related hazards.",
    "field": "Antarctic contribution to sea-level rise",
    "problem": "Current climate models inadequately represent complex interactions among the atmosphere, ocean, and cryosphere, particularly the uncertain impacts of atmospheric rivers, leading to significant uncertainty in projecting Antarctica\u2019s future contribution to global sea-level rise.",
    "method": "This perspective paper does not propose a specific method; it assesses uncertainties in sea-level rise projections and recommends future research priorities\u2014including enhanced observations and coupled modeling\u2014to better understand atmospheric and oceanic forcing on Antarctica.",
    "task": "The task is to assess Antarctica's future contribution to global sea-level rise by highlighting uncertainties from atmospheric and oceanic forcings, with a specific focus on atmospheric rivers and their consequences for projecting sea-level rise-related hazards.",
